<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nubia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nubian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nubia is the region along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, covering southern Egypt and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. It was rich in gold and controlled trade routes to tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> conquered it and governed it through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-kush">Viceroy of Kush</a>, while Nubia was also the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>, which later ruled Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nubia.jpg" alt="Nubia" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Gehricke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rulers_of_Kush,_Kerma_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibn Sa'id al-Maghribi records that the Damadim, a people from the south, attacked <b>Nubia</b> in 1220; Soba may have been conquered and destroyed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors pressed aggressively outward: Thutmose I crossed the Euphrates, the first pharaoh to do so, and carried Egyptian arms beyond the fourth Nile cataract in <b>Nubia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A new Kushite state took shape at Napata, near the holy mountain of Jebel Barkal, after Egyptian control of <b>Nubia</b> collapsed with the end of the New Kingdom around 1070 BCE and local elites reasserted independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Farmers lifted water to their fields with the saqia, an ox-driven waterwheel that spread widely in medieval <b>Nubia</b> and allowed two or three harvests a year of sorghum, barley, wheat and dates, while cattle, sheep and goats grazed beyond the floodplain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Campaigns under Baybars, Qalawun and al-Nasir Muhammad secured Syria and the Hejaz, reduced Makuria in <b>Nubia</b> to vassalage and raided Cilician Armenia, while the victory at Ain Jalut in 1260 and later at Homs and Marj al-Saffar checked Mongol expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the New Kingdom Senusret III was worshipped as a god in <b>Nubia</b>, and Greek writers knew Amenemhat III's mortuary temple at Hawara as the Labyrinth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
